const v12ReadA={id:"v12-read-a",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 69-72",title:"Why We Have Leap Years",description:"A calendar year of 365 days is a convenient approximation, but the Earth does not cooperate. It takes about 365 and one quarter days to travel once around the sun. If every year had exactly 365 days, the calendar would drift by about one day every four years, and after a few centuries the seasons would arrive weeks off schedule.\n\nThe Julian calendar, introduced in Rome in 46 BCE, fixed most of this by adding a day every fourth year. But the true length of the year is slightly less than 365.25 days, by about eleven minutes. Those minutes added up, and by the sixteenth century the calendar had drifted about ten days.\n\nIn 1582, Pope Gregory XIII introduced a correction that most of the world now uses. Ten days were dropped from the calendar at once, and the leap-year rule was refined: years divisible by four are leap years, except century years, which are leap years only if they are divisible by 400. So 1900 was not a leap year, but 2000 was.\n\nThe Gregorian calendar is still not perfect, but its error is now only about one day in more than three thousand years.",alt:"Passage on the length of the year, the Julian leap-year rule and its eleven-minute error, the 1582 Gregorian correction and its century rule."};
const v12ReadB={id:"v12-read-b",kind:"passage",label:"Babasahin para sa Aytem 73-76",title:"Ang Lupa sa Paanan ng Bulkan",description:"Nakapagtataka sa marami kung bakit may mga taong naninirahan at nagsasaka sa paanan ng mga bulkan, kahit alam nilang maaari itong pumutok. Isa sa mga dahilan ay ang lupa.\n\nKapag pumutok ang bulkan, nagbubuga ito ng abo at mga piraso ng bato na mayaman sa mineral. Sa paglipas ng panahon, dinudurog ng ulan, ng init, at ng mga halaman ang abong ito hanggang maging lupa. Kadalasang mataba ang lupang ito, dahil taglay nito ang mga mineral na kailangan ng mga pananim. Kaya masagana ang ani ng palay, gulay, at prutas sa maraming lugar na malapit sa bulkan.\n\nNgunit hindi agad-agad nagiging mataba ang lupa. Maaaring makasira muna ng mga pananim ang bagong abo at magpabara sa mga ilog. Kailangan ng maraming taon bago ito maging mabuting lupa.\n\nDahil dito, mahalaga ang pagbabantay ng mga dalubhasa sa bulkan. Sinusukat nila ang paglindol, ang pag-umbok ng lupa, at ang mga gas na lumalabas. Sa ganitong paraan, makapagbibigay sila ng babala upang makalikas ang mga residente bago pa man pumutok ang bulkan.",alt:"Tekstong pang-agham tungkol sa matabang lupa mula sa abo ng bulkan, kung bakit hindi ito agad nakabubuti, at kung paano binabantayan ang bulkan para sa babala."};
const v12ReadC={id:"v12-read-c",kind:"passage",label:"Passage for Items 77-80",title:"Sleep and Memory",description:"Students preparing for exams often treat sleep as time they can borrow from. Research on memory suggests that this trade is usually a poor one.\n\nLearning a new fact or skill is only the first step in remembering it. In the hours afterward, the brain works to stabilize the new memory, a process researchers call consolidation. Much of this work appears to happen during sleep. In laboratory studies, people who learned a list of words or a simple motor task and then slept usually performed better when tested later than people who spent the same number of hours awake.\n\nSleep also seems to prepare the brain for new learning. Volunteers who were kept awake all night generally did worse the next day at forming new memories than those who had rested.\n\nNone of this means that sleep can replace study. A person cannot remember what was never learned in the first place. But it does suggest that the student who stays up until three in the morning rereading notes may be undoing part of the work, and that a regular schedule of study followed by sleep is likely to pay off better than a final all-night effort.",alt:"Health science passage on memory consolidation during sleep, the effect of a sleepless night on new learning, and why study followed by sleep beats an all-nighter."};
